# Depth-2 sharding — 2026-08-07

Goal: get below the parallel floor. The depth-1 partition splits on the FIRST vertex type only, so it
cannot cut inside a single first-type subtree, and one always dominates: slowest shard 218 s of 1109
CPU-s at k=7 (19.7%, 200 shards) and 803 s of 4140 at k=8 (19.4%, 400 shards). A fixed FRACTION, so
more shards buy nothing — measured, not assumed.

## What was implemented

`EU_SHARD_D2=<f>` in `eu_solver.cpp` (default 1 = the old behaviour, byte-identical). EU_SHARD_N stays
the TOTAL shard count and splits two ways:

    N1 = N / D2  root slices,  w1 = W / D2     which first vertex types this shard walks
    D2           branch slices, w2 = W % D2    which of that root's level-1 branches it descends

extend() called on a one-vertex seed branches into root-level children (glue firstfree to an existing
free dart, or add a vertex type and glue to one of its darts). Those children partition the root's
subtree; every solution is either emitted AT the root level or lives under exactly one child. So
numbering the surviving root-level branches and slicing that number is exact — the depth-1 argument,
one level down. The counter is shared across roots safely because the branch sequence does not depend
on shard state: a skipped branch sets and clears the same glue as a taken one, and checkpart_inc reads
only the configuration.

Two levels rather than pure depth-2 because pure depth-2 makes EVERY shard redo EVERY root's level-1
work (writecycle + candidate scan + checkpart_inc per branch). With the split above that duplication
is D2-fold, not N-fold. `main()` refuses a run where D2 does not divide N — a remainder would leave
root slices no shard walks, i.e. silently missing tilings.

## Correctness: the tiling set is identical, the catalog TEXT is not

k=4, star24full, four partition shapes:

| shape | digest | blocks |
|---|---|---|
| sequential (N=1) | `0d6c89a535a16ad8` | 678 |
| depth-1, N=8 D2=1 | `0d6c89a535a16ad8` | 678 |
| depth-2, N=8 D2=8 | `17a1384157a0d211` | 678 |
| hybrid, N=8 D2=2 | `f7662cc5b462713d` | 678 |

Same 44/74/169/391 in every shape. The blocks differ only in the ORDER of orbits inside the vertype
line (and the `…N.tes` ordinal, which is a per-shard emission counter).

**The decisive check** is the project's own canonical form, not my reading of the diffs: concatenate
the raw output of the sequential run and the depth-2 run — 2600 raw blocks — and prune the union. It
collapses to exactly 678, 44/74/169/391. The WL canonical form identifies every depth-2 block with its
sequential counterpart, so the two runs describe the same tilings.

**Why the text moves.** The printed orbit order is the DFS insertion order, and the pruner keeps the
FIRST-SEEN representative of each isomorphism class. Depth-1 sharding preserves per-root DFS order
(each root's whole subtree is walked by one shard in one sequence), which is why 200-shard and
400-shard runs gave byte-identical catalogs. Depth-2 splits inside a root, so a tiling reachable from
two different root-level branches can now be first-found by the other one.

⚑ **This is a real migration cost, not a cosmetic one.** The atlas keys ids, the family fold and
`cells_index` by vertype STRING. Adopting depth-2 for a shipped catalog would move some of those keys.
The clean fix is to make the pruner choose a canonical representative (e.g. lex-min vertype among the
class) instead of first-seen, which would make the digest stable under ANY search order — worth doing
on its own merits, but it re-baselines every digest and is a separate decision.


